It is necessary to determine if the level of performance of the building after alteration is below that which existed before the change (refer to section 16-88). The hazard level could be increased for attributes (such as floor loads) for a given alteration. The evaluation of the change in hazard levels of each attribute can be accomplished using various tools or methods singularly or in combination as follows:
(1) Performance data on architectural and structural systems encountered in existing buildings can be compared to the proposed altered systems to determine if the performance is being adversely affected;
(2) Compliance alternatives to code noncompliance problems as set forth in various provisions of this chapter may be accepted. The alternate solutions provided in this article are not all inclusive and should not preclude consideration of other alternatives;
(3) Analytical methods based on good engineering practice may be used to determine changes in performance levels; and/or
(4) Professional judgment of the enforcement authorities based on previous experience with similar buildings, conditions or circumstances shall be used to the fullest extent possible.
(Ord. No. 83-4-5, § 1, 4-19-83; Code 1981, § 8-9005)

